<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  ICT3113 Assignment 1 — load_post_tickets.jmx
  Owner: Part 1 — Yeo Kai Yuan (test harness).  Feeds slides 8 and 9.

  The steady-state load plan: ticket narratives arrive one at a time at a fixed,
  controlled rate and are POSTed to /tickets, exactly as the client's complaint
  intake would deliver them. Nothing else runs in this plan.

  Hand-written rather than GUI-generated so that it stays reviewable in a diff.
  It still opens and re-saves cleanly in the JMeter 5.6.3 GUI. Every element
  carries a GUI-visible comment (TestPlan.comments) as well, so the reasoning
  survives a GUI round-trip.

  Run it through scripts/run_load_test.sh; see jmeter/README.md for the property
  list and for how request_id / source_row reconciliation works.
-->
<jmeterTestPlan version="1.2" properties="5.0" jmeter="5.6.3">
  <hashTree>
    <TestPlan guiclass="TestPlanGui" testclass="TestPlan" testname="ICT3113 A1 — steady load on POST /tickets" enabled="true">
      <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">WHY THIS PLAN IS OPEN-LOOP, AND WHY IT MUST STAY THAT WAY.

This plan uses an Open Model Thread Group, which is an open-loop (arrival-rate)
load model: JMeter decides in advance WHEN each ticket arrives and starts a
thread for it, whether or not the previous ticket has been answered.

A closed-loop thread group does the opposite. A fixed pool of N threads each
sends a request, waits for the response, then sends the next. The offered rate is
therefore N / response_time, so the moment the service slows down the load
generator slows down with it. Three things then go wrong at once:
  1. the test silently stops offering the rate we claim to be testing, so a
     latency figure cannot be attributed to any stated arrival rate;
  2. no queue ever builds up in front of the service or in front of Ollama, so
     the very failure mode we are looking for is hidden by the measurement;
  3. throughput appears to plateau smoothly instead of the service falling over,
     which flatters the baseline and makes the Assignment 2 comparison worthless.

The assignment brief is explicit: "Traffic must be generated open-loop, at
controlled arrival rates: use the Open Model Thread Group or the Precise
Throughput Timer... Results from closed-loop tests will not be accepted as
evidence against throughput or latency requirements."

So: no Thread Group, no Ultimate Thread Group, no Constant Throughput Timer
sitting inside a loop. If the service cannot keep up, that must show as growing
latency and rising errors, not as a load generator that quietly backs off.

BASELINE NOTE. Nothing in this plan tries to make the system under test look
fast. There is no warm-up sampler (scripts/run_load_test.sh sends one warm-up
request with X-Warmup: 1 before the run and stores its log line separately), no
think time, no caching of anything, and no reuse of a request across samples.
# A2 candidate: none in this file — the load generator is not the thing we are
# optimising. Any change here changes what we measure, not how fast the service is.</stringProp>
      <boolProp name="TestPlan.functional_mode">false</boolProp>
      <boolProp name="TestPlan.serialize_threadgroups">false</boolProp>
      <boolProp name="TestPlan.tearDown_on_shutdown">true</boolProp>
      <elementProp name="TestPlan.user_defined_variables" elementType="Arguments" guiclass="ArgumentsPanel" testclass="Arguments" testname="User Defined Variables" enabled="true">
        <collectionProp name="Arguments.arguments"/>
      </elementProp>
      <stringProp name="TestPlan.user_define_classpath"></stringProp>
    </TestPlan>
    <hashTree>
      <!--
        Open Model Thread Group. The schedule string is fixed by the build
        contract and must not be edited; change the run instead by passing
        -Jrate_per_min and -Jduration_s.
      -->
      <org.apache.jmeter.threads.openmodel.OpenModelThreadGroup guiclass="org.apache.jmeter.threads.openmodel.gui.OpenModelThreadGroupGui" testclass="org.apache.jmeter.threads.openmodel.OpenModelThreadGroup" testname="Ticket intake" enabled="true">
        <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">Schedule: rate(${__P(rate_per_min,60)}/min) random_arrivals(${__P(duration_s,300)} sec)

"random_arrivals" is a Poisson arrival process at the stated rate, which is what
a complaint intake actually looks like: requests clump, they do not arrive at
tidy equal intervals. Evenly spaced arrivals would understate the concurrency
the service really sees at a given mean rate.

Because the start and finish rates are the same, the load is flat for the whole
duration — this plan measures a steady state, not a ramp. jmeter/stress_ramp.jmx
does the ramp.

Expected samples per run is rate_per_min / 60 * duration_s, and because arrivals
are random the actual count varies a little around that. At very low rates over
very short durations the expected count is below one and a run can legitimately
produce zero samples; that is the Poisson process, not a broken plan.

on_sample_error = continue: an error is data. A failed sample must be recorded
and counted in the error rate, never allowed to stop the run, or the error rate
we report would depend on when the first failure happened.

random_seed = ${__P(random_seed,0)}: 0 means JMeter picks a fresh seed per run,
so the three repeat runs of a configuration see three different arrival patterns
and the spread we report across runs includes arrival variability.
TODO(Part 5 — Koh Tong Wei): if you would rather hold the arrival pattern fixed
across the three runs so that the spread reflects only the service, pass a
non-zero -Jrandom_seed and say so in the playbook. Do not change the default
here without recording the decision.</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="ThreadGroup.on_sample_error">continue</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="OpenModelThreadGroup.schedule">rate(${__P(rate_per_min,60)}/min) random_arrivals(${__P(duration_s,300)} sec) pause(${__P(drain_s,150)} sec)</stringProp>
        <stringProp name="OpenModelThreadGroup.random_seed">${__P(random_seed,0)}</stringProp>
        <elementProp name="ThreadGroup.main_controller" elementType="org.apache.jmeter.threads.openmodel.OpenModelThreadGroupController"/>
      </org.apache.jmeter.threads.openmodel.OpenModelThreadGroup>
      <hashTree>
        <!--
          The ticket source. Defaults to the synthetic dev tickets, never to the
          team's real rows: nothing in this repository may put a team row in
          front of a model before the golden set and the prediction record are
          committed and tagged. scripts/run_load_test.sh passes -Jinput_csv and
          only passes data/team_rows.csv once scripts/freeze_gate.py has passed.
        -->
        <CSVDataSet guiclass="TestBeanGUI" testclass="CSVDataSet" testname="Ticket narratives (CSV Data Set Config)" enabled="true">
          <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">Reads data/dev/synthetic_tickets.csv or data/team_rows.csv; both have the
columns row_number,narrative,raw_label and a header line.

quotedData=true is not optional. 379 of our 1,000 team narratives contain a
newline and 780 contain a comma, so the narrative column is a quoted CSV field
that spans several physical lines. With quoted data disabled JMeter would split
those rows at the first comma and post fragments of tickets.

ignoreFirstLine=true skips the header line; JMeter re-skips it on every recycle.

recycle=true and stopThread=false: the file is re-read from the top at EOF and no
thread is ever stopped by running out of data. A long run at a high rate needs
more samples than the file has rows, and an open-loop test must keep offering the
configured rate for the whole duration. Re-reading means a ticket can be posted
more than once in a long run, which is correct for a throughput test and is why
accuracy is measured separately by scripts/run_accuracy.py, one golden ticket at
a time, and never from a load run.

shareMode.all: one file handle shared by every thread, so each arriving ticket
takes the next row rather than every thread reading from the top.

raw_label is read but deliberately never used. The raw dataset labels are noisy —
that is the whole reason Part 2 builds a golden set — so this plan must not
compare the service's answer against raw_label anywhere.

The default path is relative to this .jmx file, because that is how JMeter
resolves relative CSV paths. run_load_test.sh passes an absolute path.</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="filename">${__P(input_csv,../data/dev/synthetic_tickets.csv)}</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="fileEncoding">UTF-8</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="variableNames">row_number,narrative,raw_label</stringProp>
          <boolProp name="ignoreFirstLine">true</boolProp>
          <stringProp name="delimiter">,</stringProp>
          <boolProp name="quotedData">true</boolProp>
          <boolProp name="recycle">true</boolProp>
          <boolProp name="stopThread">false</boolProp>
          <stringProp name="shareMode">shareMode.all</stringProp>
        </CSVDataSet>
        <hashTree/>
        <!--
          The half of the reconciliation mechanism that leaves the load
          generator: two headers carrying the per-iteration identifiers that the
          User Parameters pre-processor set. The service copies both into its
          JSONL log line, and analysis/reconcile.py joins on request_id.
        -->
        <HeaderManager guiclass="HeaderPanel" testclass="HeaderManager" testname="HTTP Header Manager — content type and reconciliation headers" enabled="true">
          <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">X-Request-ID is the join key between this .jtl and logs/service/*.jsonl. The
service reads it, uses it verbatim as the request_id in its log line, and returns
it in the response body, so one HTTP sample and one log line can always be tied
together. If the header were absent the service would generate a UUID of its own
and the two files could not be joined at all.

X-Source-Row carries the course CSV row number that this narrative came from, so
a log line can be traced back to the exact ticket text without storing the text
in the .jtl.

Content-Type must be application/json for FastAPI to parse the body.</stringProp>
          <collectionProp name="HeaderManager.headers">
            <elementProp name="Content-Type" elementType="Header">
              <stringProp name="Header.name">Content-Type</stringProp>
              <stringProp name="Header.value">application/json</stringProp>
            </elementProp>
            <elementProp name="X-Request-ID" elementType="Header">
              <stringProp name="Header.name">X-Request-ID</stringProp>
              <stringProp name="Header.value">${request_id}</stringProp>
            </elementProp>
            <elementProp name="X-Source-Row" elementType="Header">
              <stringProp name="Header.name">X-Source-Row</stringProp>
              <stringProp name="Header.value">${source_row}</stringProp>
            </elementProp>
          </collectionProp>
        </HeaderManager>
        <hashTree/>
        <HTTPSamplerProxy guiclass="HttpTestSampleGui" testclass="HTTPSamplerProxy" testname="POST /tickets" enabled="true">
          <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">The sampler name "POST /tickets" becomes the label column in the .jtl, and
analysis/ groups by it. Do not rename it.

host has no usable default. The brief requires the load generator to run on a
different machine from the service, so there is no correct localhost value to
fall back on; the default below is a deliberately invalid hostname that fails
immediately with UnknownHostException rather than quietly measuring a co-hosted
run. Pass -Jhost=&lt;service machine&gt;.

response_timeout_ms defaults to 180000, which is deliberately longer than the
service's own OLLAMA_TIMEOUT_S of 120 s. If JMeter gave up first we would record
a load-generator timeout instead of the service's real latency, and the number
would be an artefact of the test rig. A sample that does hit 180 s is a genuine
failure of the system under test to answer within three minutes.

use_keepalive is JMeter's default and is a generator-side setting: it keeps a TCP
handshake out of every measured sample. It changes nothing about the service,
which still builds a fresh httpx client and a fresh SQLite connection per
request by design.</stringProp>
          <elementProp name="HTTPsampler.Arguments" elementType="Arguments" guiclass="HTTPArgumentsPanel" testclass="Arguments" testname="Request body" enabled="true">
            <collectionProp name="Arguments.arguments">
              <elementProp name="" elementType="HTTPArgument">
                <boolProp name="HTTPArgument.always_encode">false</boolProp>
                <stringProp name="Argument.value">{"narrative": ${narrative_json}}</stringProp>
                <stringProp name="Argument.metadata">=</stringProp>
              </elementProp>
            </collectionProp>
          </elementProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.domain">${__P(host,SET-JHOST-TO-THE-SERVICE-HOST)}</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.port">${__P(port,8000)}</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.protocol">http</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.contentEncoding">UTF-8</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.path">/tickets</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.method">POST</stringProp>
          <boolProp name="HTTPSampler.postBodyRaw">true</boolProp>
          <boolProp name="HTTPSampler.follow_redirects">false</boolProp>
          <boolProp name="HTTPSampler.auto_redirects">false</boolProp>
          <boolProp name="HTTPSampler.use_keepalive">true</boolProp>
          <boolProp name="HTTPSampler.DO_MULTIPART_POST">false</boolProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.connect_timeout">${__P(connect_timeout_ms,10000)}</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.response_timeout">${__P(response_timeout_ms,180000)}</stringProp>
          <stringProp name="HTTPSampler.embedded_url_re"></stringProp>
        </HTTPSamplerProxy>
        <hashTree>
          <!--
            Reconciliation, step 1 of 2. "Update Once Per Iteration" is
            deliberately OFF; see the element comment for why.
          -->
          <UserParameters guiclass="UserParametersGui" testclass="UserParameters" testname="Per-iteration ids (request_id, source_row)" enabled="true">
            <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">Sets the two variables the Header Manager sends:
  request_id = ${__UUID()}   a fresh UUID4 for every ticket
  source_row = ${row_number} the CSV row this iteration is posting

UserParameters.per_iteration is FALSE on purpose, and that is the important
detail in this element. With it true, JMeter sets the values from an
iterationStart listener; JMeter notifies iteration listeners in reverse
registration order, so this element could fire BEFORE the CSV Data Set Config
had advanced to the current row, and source_row would then name the previous
ticket. Every reconciled row would be off by one and the mistake would be
invisible in the .jtl.

With per_iteration false the values are set from process(), i.e. as an ordinary
pre-processor immediately before the sampler runs, by which time the CSV row for
this iteration is already bound. There is exactly one sampler per iteration in
this plan, so the values are still per-iteration in effect — they are just
sequenced safely.

Verified: with this setting the source_row column of the .jtl walks the input CSV
in order and request_id is unique per sample.</stringProp>
            <collectionProp name="UserParameters.names">
              <stringProp name="37109963">request_id</stringProp>
              <stringProp name="-1111110730">source_row</stringProp>
            </collectionProp>
            <collectionProp name="UserParameters.thread_values">
              <collectionProp name="-1">
                <stringProp name="118040362">${__UUID()}</stringProp>
                <stringProp name="2006504376">${row_number}</stringProp>
              </collectionProp>
            </collectionProp>
            <boolProp name="UserParameters.per_iteration">false</boolProp>
          </UserParameters>
          <hashTree/>
          <!--
            Escaping only. This pre-processor exists because JMeter has no
            JSON-escape function; it contains no test logic and must not grow any.
          -->
          <JSR223PreProcessor guiclass="TestBeanGUI" testclass="JSR223PreProcessor" testname="JSON-escape the narrative (escaping, not logic)" enabled="true">
            <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">THIS IS ESCAPING, NOT LOGIC. It makes the narrative safe to paste into a JSON
body and does nothing else. It must not filter, shorten, retry, cache or classify
anything; if a future change needs one of those, it does not belong here.

Why it is needed at all: JMeter has no built-in JSON escape function, and our
narratives contain double quotes (123 of 1,000 team rows), newlines (379 of
1,000) and non-ASCII punctuation. Building the body by string concatenation would
produce invalid JSON and the service would answer 422 for a quarter of the corpus.

groovy.json.JsonOutput.toJson() returns the value ALREADY wrapped in double
quotes, which is why the body template is

    {"narrative": ${narrative_json}}

with no quotes of its own around the placeholder.

cacheKey is set so Groovy compiles the script once for the whole run instead of
once per sample. That is a load-generator cost, not a service optimisation:
recompiling per sample would burn CPU on the generator and inflate the latency
we attribute to the service.</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="cacheKey">ict3113-narrative-json-escape</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="filename"></stringProp>
            <stringProp name="parameters"></stringProp>
            <stringProp name="scriptLanguage">groovy</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="script">// Escaping only. See this element's comment.
// A missing 'narrative' variable means the input CSV's columns are wrong; we let
// that fail loudly at the service (HTTP 422) rather than paper over it here.
// Names no Java type on purpose: JMeter 5.6.3's Groovy cannot compile one on a
// newer JDK and the failure is silent. See jmeter/README.md, "Groovy and the JDK
// version". JsonOutput is a Groovy class, which is safe.
vars.put('narrative_json', groovy.json.JsonOutput.toJson(vars.get('narrative')))</stringProp>
          </JSR223PreProcessor>
          <hashTree/>
          <ResponseAssertion guiclass="AssertionGui" testclass="ResponseAssertion" testname="HTTP 200" enabled="true">
            <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">Asserts the response code equals 200, and nothing else.

There is deliberately NO assertion on the returned category. Checking the
category here would be measuring classification accuracy client-side, in the
middle of a timed load test, against whatever the load generator happened to be
told — which is not how accuracy is measured in this assignment. Accuracy is
measured only by scripts/run_accuracy.py against the frozen golden set in
golden/golden_set.csv, and only after scripts/freeze_gate.py passes. Asserting on
the category here would also mean a correct-but-slow service and a fast-but-wrong
service produced the same error rate, which would make the load results unreadable.

What this assertion is for: it turns a 502 from a model timeout, a 422 from a
malformed body, or a connection refusal into a failed sample, so the error rate
in the .jtl is a real error rate. The service returns 200 with
category = "UNPARSEABLE" when the model's reply cannot be mapped, and that is
correctly counted here as a success — the request was served; the reply was
merely unusable, which is an accuracy question, not a performance one.

assume_success=false: a failed assertion must show as a failed sample.</stringProp>
            <collectionProp name="Asserion.test_strings">
              <stringProp name="49586">200</stringProp>
            </collectionProp>
            <stringProp name="Assertion.test_field">Assertion.response_code</stringProp>
            <boolProp name="Assertion.assume_success">false</boolProp>
            <intProp name="Assertion.test_type">8</intProp>
            <stringProp name="Assertion.custom_message">Expected HTTP 200 from POST /tickets. A 502 is a model error or timeout, a 422 is a malformed request body, anything else is unexpected — check logs/service/*.jsonl for this request_id.</stringProp>
          </ResponseAssertion>
          <hashTree/>
        </hashTree>
      </hashTree>
      <!--
        Result writer. Inert unless -Jjtl_out is given; run_load_test.sh uses
        JMeter's own -l instead. See the element comment.
      -->
      <ResultCollector guiclass="SimpleDataWriter" testclass="ResultCollector" testname="Simple Data Writer (only when -Jjtl_out is set)" enabled="true">
        <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">scripts/run_load_test.sh writes results with JMeter's -l flag, which creates its
own result collector from the jmeter.save.saveservice.* settings in
jmeter/user.properties. That file, not this element, is the authoritative save
configuration, and it is what puts the request_id and source_row columns on the
end of every row.

This element exists so that the plan is also runnable by hand, or from the GUI,
without -l: pass -Jjtl_out=/absolute/path/results.jtl. Left unset the filename is
empty and nothing is written. Do not set both -l and -Jjtl_out for the same run
unless you actually want two copies of the results.

error_logging=false: save every sample, not only the failures. Percentiles
computed from failures alone would be meaningless.</stringProp>
        <boolProp name="ResultCollector.error_logging">false</boolProp>
        <objProp>
          <name>saveConfig</name>
          <value class="SampleSaveConfiguration">
            <time>true</time>
            <latency>true</latency>
            <timestamp>true</timestamp>
            <success>true</success>
            <label>true</label>
            <code>true</code>
            <message>true</message>
            <threadName>true</threadName>
            <dataType>true</dataType>
            <encoding>false</encoding>
            <assertions>true</assertions>
            <subresults>true</subresults>
            <responseData>false</responseData>
            <samplerData>false</samplerData>
            <xml>false</xml>
            <fieldNames>true</fieldNames>
            <responseHeaders>false</responseHeaders>
            <requestHeaders>false</requestHeaders>
            <responseDataOnError>false</responseDataOnError>
            <saveAssertionResultsFailureMessage>true</saveAssertionResultsFailureMessage>
            <assertionsResultsToSave>0</assertionsResultsToSave>
            <bytes>true</bytes>
            <sentBytes>true</sentBytes>
            <url>true</url>
            <threadCounts>true</threadCounts>
            <idleTime>true</idleTime>
            <connectTime>true</connectTime>
          </value>
        </objProp>
        <stringProp name="filename">${__P(jtl_out,)}</stringProp>
      </ResultCollector>
      <hashTree/>
    </hashTree>
  </hashTree>
</jmeterTestPlan>
